## Resume handoff

This complete workspace may be handed to another CSE builder. The receiving
builder does not need the tools that generated it and does not reconstruct the
render inputs. From the workspace root, run:

```bash
./cse-build
```

The generated command reads this workspace's recorded system, release,
toolchains, deployment paths, environment list, and Spack runtime identity. It
selects or provisions Spack, creates per-builder cache/keyring state, activates
Spack and the workspace, detects the lock checkpoint, and creates or reattaches
a release-specific tmux session. Running `./cse-build` again after a disconnect
returns to that session. Use `./cse-build shell` for a prepared shell without
tmux; if tmux is unavailable, the default command falls back to that shell.

The approved Spack runtime is:

- source: `{{ values.spack.source }}`
- version: `{{ values.spack.version }}`
- tag: `{{ values.spack.tag }}`
- commit: `{{ values.spack.commit }}`

Automatic mode follows the recorded `{{ values.spack.default_mode }}`
preference and uses the shared root
`{{ values.spack.shared_root }}` when available. If that root cannot be
provisioned, it uses
`$HOME/STACK_TESTING/spack/{{ values.spack.version }}`. Either choice must match
the identity above. To select one explicitly:

```bash
./cse-build --spack-mode shared
./cse-build --spack-mode local
```

The same entry point supports `status`, `concretize`, `verify`, `fetch`, and
`install`. It concretizes only missing environments and never replaces an
existing lock merely because the builder or Spack root changed. See the
generated `BUILDER-HANDOFF.md` for the complete handoff procedure.

Before activating Spack, the wrapper unloads any selected external compiler or
MPI module that is already active in its own process. Spack 1.2.2 otherwise
reports an already-loaded module as a failed load because `LOADEDMODULES` does
not change. The wrapper does not purge unrelated site modules and does not
change the parent login shell.
